Since Android 12 support for Camera HAL1 was removed from fw/base and fw/av.

MSM8916 and many ultra-legacy devices lacks HAL3 support in Camera HAL.
Some newer devices have a broken (or locked) by vendor HAL3 and forced to use HAL1 mode.

To bring back support for legacy HAL1 we need to do a lot (a really huge amount)
of reverts in fw/base and fw/av.
Moreover, since 14 we also need to do reverts in fw/ex, prebuilts/abi-dumps/ndk and prebuilts/sdk.
And, finally, every QPR release breaks these patches and  more and more efforts needed to bring back HAL1.

I am tired from this.

HAL3on1 is a real HAL3 implementation that uses HAL1 as camera backend
and does many things to allow legacy HAL1 implementation to be used in HAL3-only modern Android.
Additionally it can provide vendor extensions (like manual focus, ISO and exposure control) for any app using Camera2 API.

How it works?

***
Android 5-11:
CameraProvider <-> HAL1 or HAL3 <-> Camera stack backend

Android 12-15:
CameraProvider <-> HAL3 <-> Camera stack backend

Android 12-15 with HAL3on1:
CameraProvider <-> [HAL3on1 <-> HAL1] <-> Camera stack backend
***

Implemented hardware support levels are:
* LIMITED

Implemented capabilities are:
* BACKWARD_COMPATIBLE
* MANUAL_SENSOR (if manual ISO and exposure control are available)
* MANUAL_POST_PROCESSING (if manual white balance is available)

How to use?

1. Import HAL3on1 into your device tree.

2. Rename your HAL1 module in device tree like below:

-LOCAL_MODULE := camera.$(TARGET_BOARD_PLATFORM)
+LOCAL_MODULE := camera.legacy.$(TARGET_BOARD_PLATFORM)

3. Update device.mk to build both camera.$(TARGET_BOARD_PLATFORM) and camera.legacy.$(TARGET_BOARD_PLATFORM).

4. Tune HAL3on1 for your needs using next build properties:

* persist.camera.hal3on1.use_memfd [0-1]: use memfd_create() as shared memory provider.

* persist.camera.hal3on1.use_sysfs_torch [0-1]: allows to control torch faster using sysfs node instead of
opening camera and issuing "flash-mode=torch" to HAL1.
Set your sysfs path in TARGET_SYSFS_FLASH_PATH_BRIGHTNESS definition at BoardConfig.mk and update SELinux policies (if needed).

* persist.camera.hal3on1.use_preview_window_stub [0-1]: workaround for some HAL1 implementations
that want preview window to be set even in no-display mode.

* persist.camera.hal3on1.use_hwcomposer [0-1]: use hwcomposer for preview buffers.
May improve preview smoothness but doesn't work on some devices.

5. Test your camera behavior

NOTE: HAL3on1 is experimental and video recording is buggy.

Initially written for Lenovo A6010 with hope to be useful for other ultra-legacy devices.

acroreiser.
